  "description": "Code that points a package manager at a host that is not a public registry by writing the setting where the manager reads it: a heredoc, redirect or tee into a config file named exactly .npmrc, .yarnrc, pip.conf, pip.ini or .pypirc whose body (read to the heredoc delimiter) sets the registry or index URL, an echo or printf of that setting inside one pair of quotes redirected into such a file, npm, yarn or pnpm config set of the registry, pip config set of the index, or a variable holding the URL that is then written through an unquoted heredoc into such a file. Later installs of the project read the setting from that file, so they use the named host (a scoped registry setting covers its scope). Left alone: a host that IS one of the registries of npm, Yarn, PyPI or GitHub Packages (a subdomain, a port, a trailing dot and any letter case are accepted; a longer host name, or a user-info part in front of the host, is a different destination and is read), and the reserved documentation domains example.org, example.com and example.net. A third-party public mirror is read like any other non-public host. The rule reads the spelling of a statement, not the shell: a command that starts a line, or follows a chain separator or a conditional keyword with no comment marker or quote before it, is read, so a commented-out line is not matched, but a fenced command in documentation, a CI step or a test that writes a temporary config is read as the command it shows, and a Dockerfile RUN line is read. Stated limits, each not matched: a heredoc opener written before the redirect, text piped into tee, a global option that takes a value in front of the config subcommand, a URL held in a variable whose name carries none of registry, mirror or index, a setting more than eight lines into a heredoc body, a quoted command word, a command split across lines, the same redirect through an environment variable, and code that writes the file with its own file API.",
  "keywords": [],
